INT. VICTORIAN CONSERVATORY - TWILIGHT

Glass walls fogged with breath. Orchids hang in baskets. Ferns drip. Gaslights hiss in brass brackets.

LADY ARABELLA OKONKWO-VANCE, 40s, in black bombazine, stands alone among the palms, a pruning knife in her gloved hand. A single white orchid trembles on its stem.

The door scrapes. GILES FAIRWEATHER-ADEBAYO, 50s, in a doctor's frock coat, steps in with his bag. He stops, hat in hand.

GILES
Lady Arabella.

ARABELLA
Doctor.

GILES
I came as soon as I heard.

ARABELLA
Heard what?

GILES
That the Colonel is unwell.

ARABELLA
He is quite well. He is upstairs, asleep.

GILES
Then why did you send for me?

She turns the knife in her fingers. Light slides along the blade.

ARABELLA
I wished to show you the orchid.

GILES
I am no botanist.

ARABELLA
It blooms once in seven years.

GILES
(wary)
How charming.

ARABELLA
It was my husband's favourite.

Giles sets his bag on a wicker table. His hands are not quite steady.

GILES
I really ought to see the Colonel.

ARABELLA
In a moment.

She snips the orchid, cleanly, at the stem. It falls into her palm. She offers it to him.

ARABELLA (CONT'D)
For your lapel.

He takes it. Looks at it. At her. A drop of water falls from a fern onto the brim of his hat.

GILES
Arabella--

ARABELLA
Lady Arabella.

GILES
Lady Arabella. What are you doing?

ARABELLA
Giving you a flower.

She steps close. Pins the orchid to his coat with a hairpin. Her fingers linger.

ARABELLA (CONT'D)
It is poisonous, you know. The sap.

Giles looks down. A dark bead glistens on his lapel.

He steps back. Hits the wicker table. His bag spills.
